    "content": "India's EV market is growing at 50% YoY, but charging infrastructure varies wildly by city. Here's your practical guide to charging in India's major cities.\n\n## Delhi NCR\n\nDelhi has the best charging infrastructure in India, thanks to Tata Power and ChargeZone building aggressively.\n\n**Key operators:**\n- **Tata Power EV**: 50+ stations across Delhi, Gurgaon, Noida. CCS2 30-150kW. App: Tata Power EZ.\n- **ChargeZone**: Premium stations with CCS2 60-180kW. App: ChargeZone.\n- **EESL**: Government-owned, slow but reliable 15-22kW AC chargers.\n\n**Top locations:** Connaught Place (4 stations), Saket (3), Dwarka (5), Aerocity (3)\n\n**Highway tips:** Delhi-Jaipur (280km): Charge at Tata Power Neemrana (150kW, 110km from Delhi). Delhi-Agra (230km): Charge at ChargeZone Mathura (120kW).\n\n## Mumbai\n\nMumbai's density means lots of slow AC chargers but growing fast DC coverage.\n\n**Key operators:**\n- **Tata Power EV**: 40+ stations. Best coverage in Bandra, Andheri, Powai.\n- **Zeon**: Specializes in apartment complex charging.\n- **Fortum Charge & Drive**: Strong in commercial areas.\n\n**Top locations:** Andheri (5 stations), Bandra Kurla Complex (4), Powai (3), Worli (3)\n\n**Charging at home:** Most Mumbai EV owners charge overnight at apartment complexes — check if your building has a Zeon or Tata Power tie-up.\n\n## Bangalore\n\nBangalore has the most diverse charging ecosystem of any Indian city.\n\n**Key operators:**\n- **Ather Grid**: 30+ fast-charging points across the city, exclusively for Ather owners but some public CCS2.\n- **Tata Power EV**: 35+ stations covering MG Road, Indiranagar, Whitefield, Koramangala.\n- **Delta**: New entrant with 120kW CCS2 chargers.\n\n**Top locations:** Indiranagar (5), Whitefield (4), Koramangala (4), MG Road (3)\n\n**Silicon Valley tip:** Many tech parks (Manyata, Embassy Tech Village) have workplace charging. Check if your office offers free charging — common perk in Bangalore tech companies.\n\n## Hyderabad, Chennai, Pune, Ahmedabad, Kolkata\n\n**Hyderabad:** 25+ stations from Tata Power and ChargeZone. Strongest coverage: HITEC City, Gachibowli, Jubilee Hills.\n\n**Chennai:** 20+ stations, mostly Tata Power and EESL. Key locations: OMR, T Nagar, Adyar. Growing fast with Ola's EV hub.\n\n**Pune:** 15+ stations. Good Pune-Mumbai highway coverage (Tata Power at Lonavala, 150kW).\n\n**Ahmedabad:** 10+ stations. Key corridor: Ahmedabad-Gandhinagar highway has reliable CCS2 coverage.\n\n**Kolkata:** Sparse but growing. 10+ stations mostly in Salt Lake, New Town. Best for city commuting, not yet for road trips.\n\n## Which Operator App Do You Need?\n\n**Must-have apps:**\n1. **Tata Power EZ** — works in every major city\n2. **ChargeZone** — premium, reliable, highway focused\n3. **Ather Grid** — essential in Bangalore\n4. **Zeon** — useful in Mumbai apartments\n\n## Connector Types in India\n\n- **CCS2**: Standard for all new EVs (Tata, MG, BYD, Hyundai, Kia)\n- **Type 2 AC**: Common for overnight/slow charging\n- **CHAdeMO**: Older Nissan Leafs, becoming rare\n- **GB/T**: Only for Chinese imports (BYD older models)\n\n**Recommendation:** Look for CCS2 60kW+ for practical on-the-go charging. AC Type 2 is fine for overnight.\n\n## Pricing\n\n- **Fast DC (60-180kW)**: ₹12-18/kWh (ChargeZone premium: ₹18-22)\n- **AC slow**: ₹5-8/kWh\n- **Tata Power**: ₹12/kWh standard, some locations free during promotions\n- **Typical cost per full charge (40kWh battery)**: ₹200-720 depending on speed\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nIndia's EV charging is improving fast. Delhi and Bangalore are ready for road trips. Mumbai is great if you have home charging. Everything else works for city commuting but plan ahead for inter-city travel.",
